   <h1>Exercise Name: Seated Palms-Down Wrist Extension (often called the Reverse Barbell Wrist Curl). </h1>

<u><b><h4>Overview Exercise</h4></b></u>
   The exercise shown in this video is the Seated Palms-Down Wrist Extension (often called the Reverse Barbell Wrist Curl).

This is an isolation exercise specifically targeting the top/outer side of your forearms, which is often neglected compared to the inner forearm flexors.

 Target Muscles: Forearm Extensors (Extensor Carpi Radialis, Extensor Digitorum) and the Brachioradialis—highlighted in orange/red on the top of the forearms in the animation.
 Movement: A controlled extension of the wrist upwards against resistance using an overhand (pronated) grip with forearms supported on a bench.

</div></div>

<h1>How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1> 

<u><b><h4>1. Starting Position:</h4></b></u>
 Kneel or sit next to a flat weight bench.
 Rest your forearms flat across the bench, letting your wrists hang off the edge.
 Hold the weighted bar or dumbbells with an overhand grip (palms facing down) spaced about shoulder-width apart.


<u><b><h4>2. The Execution (Extension):</h4></b></u>
 Exhale and extend your wrists upward, raising the back of your hands toward the ceiling as high as you comfortably can.
 Pause briefly at the top to squeeze the top forearm muscles.


<u><b><h4>3. The Lowering Phase:</h4></b></u>
 Inhale and slowly lower the weight back down to the starting position in a smooth, controlled motion.




 <h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Balanced Forearm Development:</b> Builds the top/outer part of the forearm for complete, thick, and aesthetic arm strength.<br>
 
 <b>◉Wrist Joint Stability:</b> Strengthens the wrist extenders, which helps protect your joints during heavy bench presses, overhead presses, and push-ups.<br>
 
 <b>◉Enhanced Grip Endurance:</b> Supports tennis elbow prevention and builds functional crushing and pinch grip strength.



<h1> Recommended Sets & Repetitions</h1>

As shown directly in the video clip, the ideal volume for this exercise is:

| Parameter | Recommended Target |
| --- | --- |
| Sets | 3 Sets |
| Repetitions | 15 Reps |
| Rest Time | 45–60 seconds between sets |

> Pro Tip: Forearm extensors are relatively small muscles, so use light-to-moderate weights and strictly avoid swinging or jerking the elbows to prevent wrist strain.

